ce matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Length 9 m, width 4 m → A = 36 m². Check with an array idea: 9 rows of 4 unit squares. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about area with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nLength 9 m, width 4 m → A = 36 m². Check with an array idea: 9 rows of 4 unit squares. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would ha